---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { chartDefaults, findDerived, findMeasure, hybrid } from "../lib/config";
import { TOP_N_SHARE } from "../lib/derived";
import { MAX_MARKERS, MEASUREMENT_WARNING } from "../lib/events";
import { SERIES } from "../lib/theme";
import { ALL_OTHER } from "../components/MixChart";
import { RESERVED_LABELS } from "../components/PresetPanel";

// Thresholds come from config so the page cannot drift from what the charts apply. Prose here
// restates docs/METRICS.md for readers who are not analysts; METRICS.md is the source.
const shareGuards = findMeasure("share")?.guards ?? {};
const floor = (shareGuards.min_denominator ?? 0).toLocaleString("en-NZ");
const collapsePct = Math.round((shareGuards.min_share_of_trailing_median ?? 0) * 100);
const yoyFloor = findMeasure("yoy")?.guards?.min_denominator ?? 0;
const trailing = chartDefaults.trailing_window_months;
const topN = findDerived(TOP_N_SHARE)?.top_n;
const completeAt = Math.round(hybrid.complete_at_coverage * 100);
const warnBelow = Math.round(hybrid.warn_below_trailing_high_confidence_coverage * 100);
const colourSlots = chartDefaults.colour_slots_by_trailing;
---

<Layout title="Methodology" activeNav="method">
  <Fragment slot="masthead">
    <h1>Methodology & Metric Definitions</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Every figure on this site comes from a definition on this page. Nothing is forecast, and a missing month is
      left blank rather than filled in.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Data Grains</h2>
    <p>
      The register can be counted in three different ways. They answer different questions, and putting two of them
      on one axis gives misleading figures.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Survivors (<code>registrations_surviving</code>)</strong>: vehicles first registered in month <em>M</em> that
        are still registered as at the snapshot date. Every chart on this site uses this dataset, and each one
        says so. Older months shrink with every snapshot as vehicles are scrapped, so a share for an older month
        describes the vehicles still registered, not everything registered then: if one group is scrapped faster than
        another, its older share reads lower than it was at the time. <em>A survivors series and a flow series never
        go on the same chart.</em>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Flow (<code>registrations_flow</code>)</strong>: every first registration in month <em>M</em>,
        including vehicles since scrapped, from the Stats NZ Transport (TPT) series. This is the dataset
        for how many vehicles entered the fleet. No chart on this site uses it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stock (<code>fleet</code>)</strong>: every vehicle registered at a month end, whenever it was first
        registered. No chart on this site uses it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Vehicle Scope</h2>
    <p>
      Every dataset counts <strong>in-scope vehicles only</strong>. Out-of-scope vehicles stay in the archived
      monthly snapshot totals, marked as out of scope, but never reach the charts.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Vehicle types</strong>: passenger cars and vans, and goods vans, utes and trucks (per <code>vehicle_scope.csv</code>).
        Every other type is out, including motorcycles, mopeds, buses, motorhomes,
        trailers and caravans, and tractors and other machinery.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Entering the fleet</strong>: survivors and flow count only vehicles registered in New Zealand for the
        first time, whether new or used imports (per <code>import_status_map.csv</code>). Re-registered vehicles, which
        are returning to the register, are left out of both and count only in stock. Scratch-built vehicles are left out
        everywhere.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Core Measures</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Measure</th>
          <th>Definition</th>
          <th>Shown as</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Count</strong></td>
          <td>Number of in-scope vehicles matching the chart's filters in the month.</td>
          <td class="num">Vehicles</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Share</strong></td>
          <td>A group's count ÷ the count of all in-scope vehicles in the same month and dataset.</td>
          <td class="num">Percent (%)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{trailing}-month share</strong></td>
          <td>
            A group's count over the {trailing} months to this one ÷ all vehicles over the same months. It is not an
            average of monthly shares, which would weight a quiet month the same as a busy one.
          </td>
          <td class="num">Percent (%)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Change on the {trailing} months before</strong></td>
          <td>A group's share over the last {trailing} months minus its share over the {trailing} months before that.</td>
          <td class="num">Percentage points (pp)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>12-month running total</strong></td>
          <td>Sum of the count over the 12 months to this one, including this one.</td>
          <td class="num">Vehicles</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Change on a year ago</strong></td>
          <td>
            (this month − the same month last year) ÷ the same month last year. Not shown when last year's count is
            under {yoyFloor}.
          </td>
          <td class="num">Percent (%)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <strong>Percentage points vs percentages</strong>: a movement from 10% to 15% is always reported as <strong>+5.0 pp</strong>, never as +50%.
    </p>
    <p>
      A running total or a {trailing}-month share needs every one of its months. If one is missing, the line breaks
      there.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every chart on the site today shows a share. No live chart uses the count, the running total or the change on a
      year ago; they are defined here because the configuration allows them.
    </p>

    <h2>The Two Suppression Guards</h2>
    <p>
      On any share chart, a figure is greyed out when <strong>either</strong> of two guards trips. Greyed figures are
      drawn as a thin grey line over a shaded band, and marked “suppressed” in the tables under a chart, not dropped
      and not set to zero. The two guards catch different
      problems, so one cannot stand in for the other.
    </p>

    <h3>1. The Precision Floor (Fewer Than {floor} Vehicles)</h3>
    <p>
      When fewer than {floor} vehicles sit behind a share, each vehicle moves the percentage visibly, so the figure is
      too rough to read. For a {trailing}-month share, the floor applies to the {trailing} months' total. This is the
      guard that covers the sparse early record: every month under {floor} vehicles is before 1990.
    </p>

    <h3>2. The Representativeness Floor (A Collapse Against Recent Months)</h3>
    <p>
      A month can clear the precision floor comfortably and still describe a different population from the months
      around it. This guard trips when a month's total is below <strong>{collapsePct}% of the median</strong> of the
      previous {trailing} months. It applies to monthly shares only: a {trailing}-month share is not greyed for one
      collapsed month inside its window.
    </p>
    <div class="callout warning">
      <span class="callout-label">Case study: April 2020 lockdown</span>
      <p>
        April 2020 was New Zealand's Alert Level 4 lockdown. At the August 2026 snapshot, 1,542 vehicles first
        registered that month were still registered, against a median of 22,108 for the {trailing} months before: a
        fall of 93%. Hyundai Motor Group's share that month was 16.9% (261 of 1,542), against
        5.9% over the {trailing} months before.
      </p>
      <p>
        That 16.9% is not noise. With 261 of 1,542, chance alone would typically move the figure by about 0.95
        percentage points (its standard error), and it sat about eleven times that above normal. The month was precisely measured; it was a different mix of
        vehicles. NZ-new vehicles were 63.6% of the month against 54.3% over the {trailing} months before, trucks
        6.8% against 2.5%, and vans 6.2% against 4.5%. A precision guard cannot see this, because nothing about the
        figure is imprecise. Only a guard that compares the month with its own recent history catches it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Reading a Mix Chart</h2>
    <p>
      A mix chart shows how a total splits into groups: each line is one group's share of the month's vehicles. These
      are the only charts that carry event markers, though not every one of them does.
    </p>

    <h3>Lines and the Key</h3>
    <p>
      Up to {SERIES.length} lines get their own colour. The first {colourSlots} go to the largest groups over the
      last {trailing} months; the rest go to the groups with the highest {trailing}-month share at any point in the chart's
      period, so a group that mattered decades ago keeps a colour. Any further group is drawn as a thin grey line.
      On a chart that does not keep only its largest groups, every group with vehicles in the period shown gets a
      line, even one with none today. The
      key under the chart names every line, with its share over the last {trailing} months and the change on
      the {trailing} months before.
    </p>

    <h3>“{ALL_OTHER}”</h3>
    <p>
      A chart that keeps only its largest groups, such as the brand chart, adds every other group together into one
      grey line called <em>{ALL_OTHER}</em>. Which groups keep their own line is decided by vehicles over the
      last {trailing} months, and that choice holds across the whole chart. So a brand that was large decades ago but is
      small now sits inside {ALL_OTHER} in every year, including the years it was large.
    </p>

    <h3>Values With No Name</h3>
    <p>Three labels mark vehicles that cannot be put in a named group. They mean different things and are never merged.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>{RESERVED_LABELS.__UNDEFINED__}</strong>: the register has no value for this vehicle, or a value that means
        “none”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>{RESERVED_LABELS.__UNMAPPED__}</strong>: the register has a value, but it is not in this site's reference
        lists, for example a make missing from the brand list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>{RESERVED_LABELS.__OTHER__}</strong>: a recognised value folded together because there are too many to
        show separately. Only models use it, and no live chart shows models.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These vehicles always count in the total, so every share is out of all in-scope vehicles. They never take a
      colour or a place among the largest groups: on a chart that keeps only its largest groups they fall
      into {ALL_OTHER}, and on any other chart they are a thin grey line of their own, like any group past the
      palette. At the August 2026 snapshot, 26,427 vehicles (0.59%) had a make with no match in the brand list. On
      the segment chart, 89 vehicles are {RESERVED_LABELS.__UNDEFINED__.toLowerCase()}, at most three in a month between
      1957 and February 2022, so their line stays near zero except in greyed months, where a single vehicle out of a
      few dozen shows as a spike.
    </p>

    <h3>Event Markers</h3>
    <p>
      A marker places a dated event next to the line. It says when something happened, not that it caused what the
      line does.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Solid thin line</strong>: the date an event took effect.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dotted faint line</strong>: the date it was announced, where that is known, so the two dates can be
        compared.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Shaded band</strong>: an event that lasted a while, shaded from its start to its end.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dashed darker line</strong>: a change in how the register records vehicles, not in what people bought.
        Its entry in the list carries the warning <em>“{MEASUREMENT_WARNING}”</em>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each marker has a number above the chart, and the list under it (“events marked on the chart”) gives each
      event's title, dates, a one-line summary and its source. Events too close together to label separately share a
      number: <em>3 (2)</em> means marker 3 covers two events. Each still has its own line. A chart shows at
      most {MAX_MARKERS} numbers, grouping more events under each number on charts that cover longer periods.
    </p>
    <p>
      A chart shows only the events about what it breaks down by: an event about used imports appears on the
      chart that splits new from used, not on the segment chart. Events that affect every vehicle, such as a fuel tax change, are hidden until you
      press the button in the list that shows market-wide events. Only events checked against a source are shown,
      and none dated after the latest snapshot.
    </p>

    <h2>Derived Measures</h2>
    <h3>Market Concentration (Top-{topN} Share)</h3>
    <p>
      The combined share of the {topN} largest groups in the chart's breakdown that month, for example
      the {topN} largest makes. Which {topN} they are is worked out again every month, so the line tracks concentration rather
      than the fortunes of a fixed set. Vehicles with no recorded or recognised value never take a place, but they still count in the total. A
      falling line means the largest groups hold less of the total, so read the early decades with care: about a fifth
      of vehicles from the 1950s and 1960s have a make with no match in the brand list, which holds the line down. It
      carries both suppression guards.
    </p>

    <h3>Mild Hybrid Identification Coverage</h3>
    <p>
      The main hybrid code in NZTA's records does not say whether a hybrid is mild (MHEV) or full (FHEV). We classify
      hybrid nameplates as mild or full from manufacturer specifications in <code>mild_hybrid_models.csv</code>.
      Nameplates that the sources disagree on, or that the records cannot separate, stay unresolved rather than
      guessed. One diesel hybrid code is counted as mild by default, because since February 2024 almost all of it is
      Toyota Hilux and Land Cruiser diesels with a 48-volt mild-hybrid system; the full hybrids recorded under it are
      moved out by nameplate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coverage is <code>identified mild ÷ (identified mild + unresolved hybrids)</code>. It is a lower bound, because it
      counts every unresolved hybrid as a missed mild hybrid. It is published twice: counting every classification,
      and counting high-confidence classifications only. While the figure that counts every classification is
      under {completeAt}%, over all time or over the last {trailing} months, a chart that splits out mild hybrids labels them <em>{hybrid.labels.partial}</em> and states the coverage under the chart. A build
      warns if the high-confidence figure over the last {trailing} months falls under {warnBelow}%. No live chart splits
      out mild hybrids today.
    </p>

    <h2>Estimation Principles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No interpolation</strong>: a missing month is never filled in; the line breaks.</li>
      <li><strong>No extrapolation</strong>: charts end at the latest snapshot. Nothing is projected or forecast.</li>
      <li><strong>Greyed, not zeroed</strong>: a figure that trips a guard is greyed out, never shown as 0%.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Basis note</strong>: every chart states its dataset's definition and carries the note <em>“Registrations recorded by NZTA. Not the same as vehicle sales.”</em>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
